1
00:00:00,325 --> 00:00:01,550
Your tooth hurts.

2
00:00:01,550 --> 00:00:03,825
It is fourteen
thousand years ago.

3
00:00:03,825 --> 00:00:06,238
There is no dental
clinic down the road.

4
00:00:06,238 --> 00:00:08,475
So did people
simply do nothing?

5
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:10,450
Not always.

6
00:00:10,450 --> 00:00:12,550
At Villabruna, in
northern Italy,

7
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:14,588
researchers studied
a human molar with a

8
00:00:14,588 --> 00:00:15,600
cavity.

9
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:18,125
Inside it, they found
tiny scratches and

10
00:00:18,125 --> 00:00:19,400
chipped enamel.

11
00:00:20,150 --> 00:00:22,388
Experiments suggested
that pointed flint

12
00:00:22,388 --> 00:00:24,288
tools had scraped
the cavity while the

13
00:00:24,288 --> 00:00:25,975
person was alive.

14
00:00:25,975 --> 00:00:28,412
Think careful scraping,
not a modern

15
00:00:28,412 --> 00:00:29,850
dentist's drill.

16
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:32,662
That is evidence of
an attempt to treat a

17
00:00:32,662 --> 00:00:34,075
damaged tooth.

18
00:00:34,075 --> 00:00:36,550
It does not tell us
exactly how it felt,

19
00:00:36,550 --> 00:00:38,475
who helped, or
whether the treatment

20
00:00:38,475 --> 00:00:39,325
worked.

21
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:41,875
The person holding
their cheek here is

22
00:00:41,875 --> 00:00:42,975
imaginary.

23
00:00:42,975 --> 00:00:44,850
The ancient
tooth is real.

24
00:00:44,850 --> 00:00:47,500
Our ancestors
had serious skills.

25
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:49,875
I would still rather
book an appointment.
